WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.503 --> 00:00:03.548
- This idea that things are just fixed

00:00:03.548 --> 00:00:05.656
is proven not to be the case.

00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:07.430
You can get better at
things, you can grow.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.589
No matter what obstacles you face in life,

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:14.042
if you approach it with
courage and conviction,

00:00:14.042 --> 00:00:15.321
you can find a way out of it.

00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.777
But the only way you'll
reach any height in life

00:00:17.777 --> 00:00:22.099
and in love is by taking that
chance that you might fall.

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:23.841
You have to give your all.

00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:27.169
- He's an American singer,
songwriter, musician and actor.

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:30.576
He's won 10 Grammy Awards,
one Golden Globe Award,

00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:32.197
and one Academy Award.

00:00:32.197 --> 00:00:35.239
In 2007 he won the Hal
David Starlight Award

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:37.197
from the Songwriter's Hall of Fame.

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:38.736
He's John Legend, and here's my take

00:00:38.736 --> 00:00:40.795
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:40.795 --> 00:00:42.405
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:42.405 --> 00:00:44.623
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:46.078
And as always, guys, if you hear something

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.135
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:00:48.135 --> 00:00:50.455
down in the comments below,
put quotes around it as well

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:52.275
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:54.139
Also, when you write it
down, you're much more likely

00:00:54.139 --> 00:00:57.639
to lock it in for yourself as well, enjoy.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.802
(bold, upbeat music)

00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:12.534
- The thing is, every
successful person you know

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:16.034
has failed at what they love to do before.

00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:20.996
I've been unsuccessful in my career.

00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:22.812
You just didn't know about it
because I wasn't famous yet

00:01:22.812 --> 00:01:24.320
and I was trying to be famous.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.985
I was trying to get a
record deal for six years

00:01:26.985 --> 00:01:29.448
and I kept getting turned down.

00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:32.259
And any successful person you
know, even though it seems

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:34.420
like everything just happened for them,

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.547
it usually was preceded
by a bunch of failure,

00:01:37.547 --> 00:01:39.150
or at least a significant amount.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.011
But the great ones aren't
the ones that don't fail,

00:01:42.011 --> 00:01:43.983
they're the ones that
learn from their failure

00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:45.346
and they keep going and they're persistent

00:01:45.346 --> 00:01:46.715
and they're tenacious.

00:01:46.715 --> 00:01:49.933
And for me, I was trying
to get a record deal

00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:51.350
from '98 to 2004,

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:57.144
and I got turned down by
every major record label,

00:01:57.144 --> 00:02:00.667
including the one I'm signed to now.

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.687
For me, that means that
even when you hear no,

00:02:03.687 --> 00:02:05.239
you have go keep going
through all those nos

00:02:05.239 --> 00:02:06.542
until you get to yes.

00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:08.320
- A lot of people think they're prepared,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.141
but you may think they're not prepared,

00:02:10.141 --> 00:02:11.285
so what does prepared mean?

00:02:11.285 --> 00:02:14.195
- Prepared means, like I
said, you got to be great

00:02:14.195 --> 00:02:17.285
at whatever you're doing,
even if you're not known

00:02:17.285 --> 00:02:19.802
for it yet, you have to be developing

00:02:19.802 --> 00:02:21.637
into someone who can be great.

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:23.890
If you read Malcolm
Gladwell, for instance,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.040
he believes you have to
practice 10,000 hours

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.878
to be a genius at any particular field.

00:02:28.878 --> 00:02:30.929
I don't know if the exact number's right,

00:02:30.929 --> 00:02:34.184
but I'm sure directionally that's correct.

00:02:34.184 --> 00:02:37.713
You need to spend a hell of
a lot of time doing something

00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:40.320
and practicing at it
and getting better at it

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.564
for you to be great.

00:02:42.564 --> 00:02:46.147
And for a songwriter or a singer,

00:02:46.147 --> 00:02:48.527
that means writing, that means listening,

00:02:48.527 --> 00:02:51.817
that means performing,
that means practicing,

00:02:51.817 --> 00:02:53.535
that means all those things.

00:02:53.535 --> 00:02:55.009
And if you're not doing that,

00:02:55.009 --> 00:02:56.818
you're not going to become great at it.

00:02:56.818 --> 00:03:00.620
We're always thinking about what it means

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.025
to be authentic as an
artist and what it means

00:03:04.025 --> 00:03:06.266
to be authentic and to be successful

00:03:06.266 --> 00:03:08.816
and commercially viable.

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:11.657
The goal is to not let the commercial

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:14.056
invade your creative process too much.

00:03:14.056 --> 00:03:17.054
The goal is to just try
to write a great song,

00:03:17.054 --> 00:03:18.645
just try to make a great record,

00:03:18.645 --> 00:03:19.978
just try to make a great album,

00:03:19.978 --> 00:03:22.866
and then the commercial side
will take care of itself.

00:03:22.866 --> 00:03:26.357
But you know, you still have labels,

00:03:26.357 --> 00:03:28.409
A&amp;R is in your ear, you
still have marketing people

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:30.511
in your ear that want a certain thing out

00:03:30.511 --> 00:03:32.374
of your first single and
want a certain thing out

00:03:32.374 --> 00:03:35.155
of your first video, but
at the end of the day,

00:03:35.155 --> 00:03:39.564
your guiding light, your
North Star has to be,

00:03:39.564 --> 00:03:41.228
is this great, is this creatively

00:03:41.228 --> 00:03:43.691
something I can be proud of?

00:03:43.691 --> 00:03:44.771
Because if you don't do that,

00:03:44.771 --> 00:03:46.580
then I don't know what the point is.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.399
When I was younger, I didn't really know

00:03:48.399 --> 00:03:51.027
that you could just get better at things,

00:03:51.027 --> 00:03:53.007
because I think we're so used to the idea

00:03:53.007 --> 00:03:54.878
that you're talented at something

00:03:54.878 --> 00:03:57.628
and it's sort of fixed, you know?

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:02.894
The idea that oh, he's just a good singer,

00:04:02.894 --> 00:04:05.561
you know, and even with singing,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.083
I've gotten better at
singing as I've gotten older.

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:11.966
I wasn't singing in a
way that was sustainable

00:04:11.966 --> 00:04:14.329
and I wasn't breathing right,

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:16.292
I wasn't doing a bunch
of things correctly.

00:04:16.292 --> 00:04:19.501
I realized that there was a ceiling

00:04:19.501 --> 00:04:23.316
that I couldn't crack through
unless I actually studied

00:04:23.316 --> 00:04:26.356
and worked under a coach to get better.

00:04:26.356 --> 00:04:29.689
And this idea that things are just fixed

00:04:30.835 --> 00:04:32.869
is proven not to be the case.

00:04:32.869 --> 00:04:35.145
You can get better at
things, you can grow.

00:04:35.145 --> 00:04:37.899
And even people that are
innately talented still need

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:41.076
to go beyond that basic talent

00:04:41.076 --> 00:04:43.173
that they have and cultivate it.

00:04:43.173 --> 00:04:46.399
I love what I do, I'm a musician.

00:04:46.399 --> 00:04:47.851
There's an industry side of it,

00:04:47.851 --> 00:04:50.743
but at the core what I do is,

00:04:50.743 --> 00:04:53.331
I go in and write songs
and I get to perform them

00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:57.604
in front of thousands of
people who enjoy them.

00:04:57.604 --> 00:05:02.262
That is fun (laughing), that is great.

00:05:02.262 --> 00:05:04.762
There are challenges involved.

00:05:05.662 --> 00:05:09.006
You want to do well, you want
to keep the public's interest

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:11.732
and their excitement,
you want to sell tickets,

00:05:11.732 --> 00:05:14.836
you want to sell CDs, you want to continue

00:05:14.836 --> 00:05:17.586
to be successful in the industry.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:21.326
But, like I said before, I
don't see a contradiction

00:05:21.326 --> 00:05:23.106
between doing all of that and having fun

00:05:23.106 --> 00:05:25.773
and making music you believe in.

00:05:27.232 --> 00:05:29.176
I don't know, I hear people complaining

00:05:29.176 --> 00:05:30.882
about the music business, and
there are certainly things

00:05:30.882 --> 00:05:34.876
that you could complain about,
but I'm having a great time.

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:38.309
I want to win, I want to
be successful at what I do.

00:05:38.309 --> 00:05:39.990
And so I'm committed to working hard.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.485
I'm committed to going to the studio

00:05:42.485 --> 00:05:47.033
and working on a song the
umpteenth version of it

00:05:47.033 --> 00:05:49.471
to make sure I get it exactly right.

00:05:49.471 --> 00:05:50.921
I'm committed to that, I'm
committed to rehearsing

00:05:50.921 --> 00:05:53.073
with my band til we get
the show exactly right.

00:05:53.073 --> 00:05:56.273
We want to do the best
show in the business.

00:05:56.273 --> 00:05:57.650
Part of it's just my love for music.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:59.868
Part of it's me being competitive.

00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:02.949
But all of that makes me want
to perform at a high level.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:05.174
- John, why this project,
why was it so important

00:06:05.174 --> 00:06:06.380
to be a part of it?

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.253
- Well, they sent me the first script

00:06:09.253 --> 00:06:13.549
and I was just enthralled,
I was like excited

00:06:13.549 --> 00:06:15.205
to find out where it went.

00:06:15.205 --> 00:06:16.459
I thought it was an exciting story

00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:18.976
that hasn't been told in this way before.

00:06:18.976 --> 00:06:22.854
And I thought it was
inspirational about breaking free,

00:06:22.854 --> 00:06:26.871
about having the courage to
do something against the odds.

00:06:26.871 --> 00:06:28.716
- So, congratulations on the baby girl!

00:06:28.716 --> 00:06:29.819
- Thank you very much.

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:31.481
- If your daughter sees
this, what do you want her

00:06:31.481 --> 00:06:32.660
to take away from this?

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:34.255
- I want her to be inspired, to know a bit

00:06:34.255 --> 00:06:35.963
about American history and know about

00:06:35.963 --> 00:06:38.128
where her father's people came from.

00:06:38.128 --> 00:06:40.045
And also to be inspired

00:06:41.607 --> 00:06:45.343
that no matter what
obstacles you face in life,

00:06:45.343 --> 00:06:48.940
if you approach it with
courage and conviction,

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.829
you can find a way out of it.

00:06:50.829 --> 00:06:52.826
I'm the kind of artist
that doesn't fit so neatly

00:06:52.826 --> 00:06:55.274
into a radio format.

00:06:55.274 --> 00:06:57.857
And that presents a challenge.

00:06:57.857 --> 00:07:00.690
But I think it's one that's worth,

00:07:01.895 --> 00:07:05.059
it's worth it for me to
be that kind of artist

00:07:05.059 --> 00:07:08.642
because I think it's
authentic to who am I.

00:07:10.547 --> 00:07:13.755
And if you're not being
authentic to who you are,

00:07:13.755 --> 00:07:16.228
then you won't be
successful anyway usually.

00:07:16.228 --> 00:07:20.072
And I think that's why
I've been successful,

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:22.102
because my fans believe what I'm doing

00:07:22.102 --> 00:07:25.598
and they believe that I'm
coming from a real place.

00:07:25.598 --> 00:07:27.807
Also, most of my songs are in some way

00:07:27.807 --> 00:07:29.476
a co-write of some sort.

00:07:29.476 --> 00:07:31.375
I usually write most of
the lyric and melody,

00:07:31.375 --> 00:07:35.055
but a lot of times the
music and the back beat

00:07:35.055 --> 00:07:37.722
are contributed by someone else,

00:07:38.769 --> 00:07:40.953
or at least in collaboration
with someone else.

00:07:40.953 --> 00:07:43.114
And I've always been open
to that, and it helps me

00:07:43.114 --> 00:07:46.197
kind of stay creative and stay fresh.

00:07:47.736 --> 00:07:50.419
And I think I come into a collaboration

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:54.661
with a spirit of openness
that not every artist has.

00:07:54.661 --> 00:07:58.303
Some people are more
guarded and more protective

00:07:58.303 --> 00:08:00.260
about their creative process,

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.721
but I'm much more open and collaborative.

00:08:03.721 --> 00:08:05.811
And for me, that's the best way to create.

00:08:05.811 --> 00:08:08.480
Everybody has a different sweet spot,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.187
and whatever makes them happy,

00:08:10.187 --> 00:08:11.789
whatever makes them most comfortable,

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:13.876
but for me, I feel very comfortable

00:08:13.876 --> 00:08:15.044
in a collaborative environment.

00:08:15.044 --> 00:08:18.252
I treat it with humility in the sense

00:08:18.252 --> 00:08:20.669
that I'm not wed to my ideas.

00:08:23.163 --> 00:08:26.496
I'm willing to listen to someone else's.

00:08:27.847 --> 00:08:31.597
I think I have a decent
sense of taste enough

00:08:32.699 --> 00:08:35.891
to be able to kind of appraise
whether or not something

00:08:35.891 --> 00:08:37.994
that someone else is
contribution is really quality

00:08:37.994 --> 00:08:41.494
and good, and I enjoy that back and forth.

00:08:43.232 --> 00:08:45.430
- Well, I think that, again,
speaks to great confidence.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.501
Because I'm a writer, and I
don't show my work to anybody

00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:49.010
because I'm so scared.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.519
If they like it, I don't trust them.

00:08:50.519 --> 00:08:52.674
If they don't like it,
I really get hurt by it.

00:08:52.674 --> 00:08:56.257
- Yeah, you have to
open yourself up to it,

00:08:57.368 --> 00:09:01.132
and part of it is just
being a little bit fearless,

00:09:01.132 --> 00:09:05.003
you know, I don't know if you
can be a little bit fearless.

00:09:05.003 --> 00:09:07.419
But, you know, you just
have to put it out there

00:09:07.419 --> 00:09:09.159
and not be afraid.

00:09:09.159 --> 00:09:11.913
Somehow they've scientifically determined

00:09:11.913 --> 00:09:16.822
that the public is only capable
of liking the same 10 songs

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:20.724
at any given time, so they
simply play those songs

00:09:20.724 --> 00:09:23.391
over and over and over and over,

00:09:27.028 --> 00:09:30.465
until you're finally
completely exasperated.

00:09:30.465 --> 00:09:32.324
Then they move on.

00:09:32.324 --> 00:09:35.883
Now, I've had a 10 year
career as a major label

00:09:35.883 --> 00:09:38.726
solo artist, and none of my songs has ever

00:09:38.726 --> 00:09:42.893
been one of those 10 songs
until this moment, and now,

00:09:44.814 --> 00:09:48.814
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:09:49.948 --> 00:09:53.529
and now (singing) all of you,

00:09:53.529 --> 00:09:56.626
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:09:56.626 --> 00:09:57.876
are so over me.

00:09:59.037 --> 00:10:00.905
(crowd laughing)

00:10:00.905 --> 00:10:05.152
You're tired of hearing
that I went to Penn.

00:10:05.152 --> 00:10:07.692
Why'd they bring him back again?

00:10:07.692 --> 00:10:10.442
(crowd laughing)

00:10:11.976 --> 00:10:14.107
That was my humble brag way of saying

00:10:14.107 --> 00:10:16.326
I have the biggest song
in the country right now.

00:10:16.326 --> 00:10:18.934
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:10:18.934 --> 00:10:21.826
The reason I'm here, the reason I've had

00:10:21.826 --> 00:10:25.993
such a wonderful journey so
far, is that I found love.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.860
Yes, love, we were all made to love,

00:10:31.378 --> 00:10:34.195
and I found that we live our best lives,

00:10:34.195 --> 00:10:36.712
we are at our most successful,

00:10:36.712 --> 00:10:40.065
not simply because we're smarter,

00:10:40.065 --> 00:10:42.456
or because we hustle harder,

00:10:42.456 --> 00:10:46.456
not because we become
millionaires more quickly.

00:10:46.456 --> 00:10:48.836
The key to success,
the key to happiness is

00:10:48.836 --> 00:10:51.753
opening your mind and your heart to love,

00:10:51.753 --> 00:10:55.027
spending your time doing things you love

00:10:55.027 --> 00:10:57.162
and with people you love.

00:10:57.162 --> 00:11:01.285
Even though we're made to love,
we're often afraid to love.

00:11:01.285 --> 00:11:03.183
We're afraid of being hurt deeply,

00:11:03.183 --> 00:11:04.834
afraid of feeling the pain I went through

00:11:04.834 --> 00:11:08.155
when my parents were divorced,

00:11:08.155 --> 00:11:10.384
but you're never going
to really love something

00:11:10.384 --> 00:11:13.995
or someone unless you
put those fears aside.

00:11:13.995 --> 00:11:16.816
Don't hold back, being in love means

00:11:16.816 --> 00:11:19.434
being ready to give freely and openly

00:11:19.434 --> 00:11:21.084
and being ready to risk something,

00:11:21.084 --> 00:11:22.769
risking pain and disappointment,

00:11:22.769 --> 00:11:25.768
conquering your fears and becoming anew.

00:11:25.768 --> 00:11:28.092
Alice Walker once said,

00:11:28.092 --> 00:11:31.981
"The more I wonder, the more I love."

00:11:31.981 --> 00:11:34.937
Love calls you to open your eyes to seek,

00:11:34.937 --> 00:11:36.687
to search, to wonder.

00:11:37.658 --> 00:11:39.639
Love is all-consuming,
it infiltrates your body

00:11:39.639 --> 00:11:43.121
and allows you to experience bliss,

00:11:43.121 --> 00:11:45.640
joy and true friendship.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.798
You'll be more disappointed
when something goes wrong.

00:11:47.798 --> 00:11:50.598
You might fall harder, but
the only way you'll reach

00:11:50.598 --> 00:11:53.136
any height in life and in love is

00:11:53.136 --> 00:11:56.532
by taking that chance that you might fall.

00:11:56.532 --> 00:11:59.009
You have to give your all.

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:01.743
Yes, I've been no so subtly
working in my song lyrics

00:12:01.743 --> 00:12:03.200
in my speech today.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.625
(crowd laughing)

00:12:05.625 --> 00:12:08.730
And some might think
it's all a bit too much.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.062
Here I am this R&amp;B singer
with an album called,

00:12:11.062 --> 00:12:14.009
Love in the Future, who's recently married

00:12:14.009 --> 00:12:16.452
and wrote the biggest
love song of the year.

00:12:16.452 --> 00:12:18.378
And what did I choose to talk about?

00:12:18.378 --> 00:12:20.878
Love, it's so corny, isn't it?

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.580
(crowd laughing)

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:27.551
It's much cooler to be
detached and apathetic, right?

00:12:27.551 --> 00:12:30.808
We all like a little snark
and cynicism and irony,

00:12:30.808 --> 00:12:32.778
especially from our favorite artists

00:12:32.778 --> 00:12:34.543
and comedians and writers.

00:12:34.543 --> 00:12:38.710
I get it, but that cool
detachment only gets you so far.

00:12:39.565 --> 00:12:42.671
Passion gets you a lot further.

00:12:42.671 --> 00:12:45.927
It makes you a better
entrepreneur, a better leader,

00:12:45.927 --> 00:12:50.094
a better philanthropist, a
better friend, a better lover.

00:12:51.667 --> 00:12:54.760
I want you to live the best life you can.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.927
You can be world changers.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.889
When you leave here today
you're going to look

00:12:59.889 --> 00:13:02.922
for a lot of things, security, money,

00:13:02.922 --> 00:13:06.089
friendships, sex, all kinds of things.

00:13:07.031 --> 00:13:10.056
But the most important
thing you'll find is love.

00:13:10.056 --> 00:13:12.861
So love yourself, love your work,

00:13:12.861 --> 00:13:15.633
love the people around
you, dare to love those

00:13:15.633 --> 00:13:18.854
who are different from you,
no matter where they're from,

00:13:18.854 --> 00:13:21.535
what they look like and who they love.

00:13:21.535 --> 00:13:25.535
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:13:30.266 --> 00:13:34.939
Pursue this life of love
with focus and passion,

00:13:34.939 --> 00:13:38.189
ambition and courage, give it your all,

00:13:39.223 --> 00:13:42.806
and that will be your
path to true success.

00:13:45.078 --> 00:13:46.495
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:46.495 --> 00:13:49.460
I made this video because
Funky Fun Me asked me to.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.242
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:13:51.242 --> 00:13:53.207
to profile next, leave
down in the comments below

00:13:53.207 --> 00:13:54.558
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:54.558 --> 00:13:56.806
I'd also love to know, what
did you learn from this video

00:13:56.806 --> 00:13:59.502
from John that you're going to
take some immediate action on

00:13:59.502 --> 00:14:01.856
in your life or in your
businesses somehow?

00:14:01.856 --> 00:14:04.005
What was your favorite clip and why?

00:14:04.005 --> 00:14:05.738
Leave it in the comments
and I'm going to join

00:14:05.738 --> 00:14:06.800
in the discussion.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:08.267
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:14:08.267 --> 00:14:10.798
to Rey Peterson, Rey
actually works on my team

00:14:10.798 --> 00:14:12.670
and he decided to pick
up a copy of my book,

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.521
Your One Word, thank you for that Rey.

00:14:14.521 --> 00:14:16.908
And for taking the picture
and posting it online.

00:14:16.908 --> 00:14:19.156
I appreciate all the work you do,

00:14:19.156 --> 00:14:20.670
but also the support with the book.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:22.155
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:22.155 --> 00:14:23.699
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:14:23.699 --> 00:14:27.170
to believe in yourself, and
whatever your one word is,

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.587
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:36.992
- Yeah, I worked for a company called

00:14:36.992 --> 00:14:39.997
the Boston Consulting Group,
we did management consulting.

00:14:39.997 --> 00:14:41.850
- [Interviewer] Was it
fun for you, was it nice?

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:44.479
- It was interesting, I had fun sometimes

00:14:44.479 --> 00:14:47.593
and I was bored sometimes,
I was tired sometimes

00:14:47.593 --> 00:14:49.385
because at the same time I was trying

00:14:49.385 --> 00:14:52.552
to make a demo record and playing live

00:14:53.581 --> 00:14:55.800
and doing all these other
things for my music career.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.191
But during the day I would be at work.

00:14:58.191 --> 00:15:00.690
It was an interesting
time, but I learned a lot,

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:02.940
actually, from being there.

00:15:04.181 --> 00:15:05.411
I needed to do it anyway because I needed

00:15:05.411 --> 00:15:07.056
to make money somehow.

00:15:07.056 --> 00:15:08.589
I mean, I could have gotten another job,

00:15:08.589 --> 00:15:10.905
but either way it was going
to take money and time

00:15:10.905 --> 00:15:12.655
to invest to do that.

00:15:14.363 --> 00:15:17.915
So, it worked out, I have
some friends from there

00:15:17.915 --> 00:15:19.460
that I'm still very good friends with.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:21.127
And I learned a lot there.

00:15:21.127 --> 00:15:24.460
- [Interviewer] What did you
learn, what did you learn?

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:25.844
- It's not a specific
fact, it's just kind of

00:15:25.844 --> 00:15:27.578
how businesses run, how they think,

00:15:27.578 --> 00:15:30.995
how to do problem solving in a smart way.

00:15:34.376 --> 00:15:38.506
And I think those things and the instincts

00:15:38.506 --> 00:15:41.509
that I developed as a business person,

00:15:41.509 --> 00:15:44.579
they help as I'm running my business now.

00:15:44.579 --> 00:15:47.659
Because really all artists are
in some ways entrepreneurs.

00:15:47.659 --> 00:15:49.500
They run their own small business.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:52.135
- [Interviewer] So, is there one client

00:15:52.135 --> 00:15:54.348
that you worked for that you said,

00:15:54.348 --> 00:15:56.020
I had to solve one problem over there

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.861
and now when I run my own
business I ran into it

00:15:58.861 --> 00:16:01.767
and I learned and I used the experience

00:16:01.767 --> 00:16:04.482
to solve my own problems?

00:16:04.482 --> 00:16:06.291
- It's not that easy to draw a line

00:16:06.291 --> 00:16:09.318
between the experiences, but I think

00:16:09.318 --> 00:16:10.949
when you have a general knowledge

00:16:10.949 --> 00:16:13.188
and you develop general
instincts about certain things,

00:16:13.188 --> 00:16:15.075
it just helps your decision making.

00:16:15.075 --> 00:16:17.145
And I think more than
anything that's what happened.

00:16:17.145 --> 00:16:20.228
Yeah, you just have to, it's not easy

00:16:21.669 --> 00:16:24.612
to see beyond where you are,

00:16:24.612 --> 00:16:26.165
but you have to try to
see beyond where you are,

00:16:26.165 --> 00:16:28.192
set goals beyond where you are.

00:16:28.192 --> 00:16:30.785
Think about ways that you can break out

00:16:30.785 --> 00:16:33.197
and make a difference,
and that's what I did.

00:16:33.197 --> 00:16:35.447
I had some great people
around me, my family,

00:16:35.447 --> 00:16:38.182
teachers, people that believed in me

00:16:38.182 --> 00:16:41.190
and encouraged me, sent me
off to an Ivy League school

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.419
and I figured it out after that.

00:16:43.419 --> 00:16:46.335
A lot of people want to be
stars, so the labels are

00:16:46.335 --> 00:16:48.904
in a position where they
have to and should say no

00:16:48.904 --> 00:16:51.621
to most people that come into
their door, and they need

00:16:51.621 --> 00:16:54.480
to be really sure about
you before they sign you.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:58.156
So, they said no to me a
lot, they said no to me.

00:16:58.156 --> 00:17:01.556
Even the label I'm
signed to, Columbia/Sony,

00:17:01.556 --> 00:17:06.473
turned me down like a year
or so before I got signed.

00:17:06.473 --> 00:17:10.033
So it just shows you that
you have to be persistent.

00:17:10.033 --> 00:17:13.587
You have to keep trying to get better,

00:17:13.587 --> 00:17:15.754
learn from your criticisms

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:20.397
and then just play to win.

00:17:20.397 --> 00:17:22.730
And that's what I was doing.

